enum HelpText {
    /// `theonotes help all`: the whole reference.
    static let all: String = ##"""
OVERVIEW: Read and write your notes from the command line.

USAGE: theonotes [--version] <subcommand>

OPTIONS:
  -v, --version           Print the version.
  -h, --help              Show help.

SUBCOMMANDS:
  list                    List notes, narrowed by location or one tag.
  search                  Find notes with the search query language.
  cat                     Print a note's text.
  show                    Print a note's metadata.
  outline                 Show a note's sections, each with its address.
  search-in               Find a string inside one note.
  create                  Create a note.
  append                  Add text to a note or to one of its sections.
  edit                    Replace, insert or delete exact text in a note.
  overwrite               Replace a note's text, or one section of it.
  tags                    Work with tags.
  attachments             Work with a note's attachments.
  pin                     Work with pins.
  trash                   Send a note to the trash.
  archive                 Send a note to the archive.
  restore                 Bring a note back from the trash or archive.
  app                     Talk to the running app.
  mcp-server              Serve the library to MCP clients over stdio.

HELP:
  theonotes -h, theonotes help       This overview.
  theonotes help <subcommand>        Help for one command.
  theonotes help all                 Every command's help in one document.
  theonotes --generate-completion-script <shell>
                                     Completion script for zsh, bash or fish.

OUTPUT:
  --format tsv     Tab-separated rows without a header (default).
  --format csv     RFC 4180 rows with a header.
  --format json    One JSON document on stdout, errors included.
  --fields a,b     Pick fields: comma-separated names, or `all`. The defaults depend on the command.

  JSON shapes: a list of results is `[{...},{...}]` (empty: `[]`), a single result is `{...}`,
  an error is `{"error":{"code":"...","message":"..."}}` on stdout.
  Exceptions: cat gives `{"content":"..."}`, attachments save gives
  `{"filename":...,"size":N,"base64":"..."}`.

  Commands that change something print nothing on success and take no --format;
  the exit status is the result. MCP tools return structured results for writes.

NOTE FIELDS:
  id, title, locked, tags, length (UTF-8 bytes), created, modified, pins, location
  (notes, trash or archive), todos (open tasks), done (checked tasks), attachments.
  `content` is only printed when named, and `all` does not include it: use `--fields all,content`.

CONVENTIONS:
  Exit status: 0 success, 1 the operation failed, 64 the command line is wrong.
  Errors: plain text on stderr; with --format json a JSON object on stdout. The status is the same.
  Tags: #one, #several words#, #parent/child. The leading # is optional on input.
  Timestamps: ISO 8601 in UTC (YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ).
  Escapes: TSV cells and text options use \n \r \t \\. Text read from stdin is taken literally.
  --content: read from stdin when the option is absent.
  Notes are selected by id or by --title (any case).
  Locked notes never give up their text through the CLI.

SECTION ADDRESSES:
  A section is a heading and everything below it. Its address is the heading line as written,
  `#` marks included; case matters, spacing does not. Several heading lines joined by `\n`
  spell out a path from an outer heading to an inner one. A heading that occurs twice needs
  its parents, or a last line holding a 1-based number that picks the Nth match. A last line
  `preamble` means the text under a heading, before its first subheading. `theonotes outline`
  lists every address of a note, and a wrong address is answered with candidates.
    "## Usage"                the one section with that heading
    "# Guide\n## Usage"       `## Usage` below `# Guide`
    "# Guide\n## Usage\n2"    the second `## Usage` below `# Guide`
    "## Usage\npreamble"      the text under `## Usage`, before its first subheading

MARKDOWN:
  Notes are Markdown with these extras: ==highlight== (==🔴text== and 🔵 🟢 🟡 🟣 for colours),
  ~underline~, ~~strikethrough~~, #tags, [[Note Title]] and [[Note Title|label]] links,
  $inline$ and $$block$$ math, - [ ] and - [x] tasks, pipe tables (a pipe at both ends of every row),
  callouts (> [!NOTE], also TIP, IMPORTANT, WARNING, CAUTION) and YAML front matter on the first line.
  Switched off: setext headings, indented code blocks and lazy continuation. Long lines soft-wrap.

GLOBAL OPTIONS:
  --data-dir <path>       Use the library in <path> (also THEONOTES_DATA_DIR).

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES LIST

OVERVIEW: List notes, narrowed by location or one tag.

USAGE: theonotes list [--sort <sort>] [--limit <limit>] [--offset <offset>] [--location <location>] [--tag <tag>] [--count] [--format <format>] [--fields <fields>]

OPTIONS:
  -s, --sort <sort>       Sort keys, comma-separated, each optionally :asc or
                          :desc. Keys: pinned, modified, created, title.
                          (default: pinned,modified)
  -n, --limit <limit>     Return at most this many notes. 0 prints nothing
                          (use --count for the number).
  -o, --offset <offset>   Skip this many notes first. (default: 0)
  -l, --location <location>
                          Which notes: notes, trash, archive or all. (default:
                          notes)
  --tag <tag>             Only notes with this tag or one of its nested
                          children.
  --count                 Print only how many notes match.
  --format <format>       Output format: tsv, csv or json. (default: tsv)
  --fields <fields>       Fields to print: comma-separated names, or `all`.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

FIELDS:
  Default: id, title, tags, length
  All:     id, title, locked, tags, length, created, modified, pins, location, todos, done, attachments
  Also on request: content (name it, or use --fields all,content).

RESULT:
  No match: `[]` on stdout with --format json, otherwise `No notes found.` on stderr. The status is 0 either way.
  For negation, several tags or dates use `search`.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes list
  theonotes list --tag work
  theonotes list --tag work --sort modified:asc --fields id,title,modified
  theonotes list -n 20 --format json --fields all
  theonotes list --count

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES SEARCH

OVERVIEW: Find notes with the search query language.

USAGE: theonotes search [<query-arg>] [--query <query>] [--sort <sort>] [--limit <limit>] [--offset <offset>] [--location <location>] [--count] [--format <format>] [--fields <fields>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <query-arg>             The query (see SEARCH SYNTAX). A query that starts
                          with a dash goes through --query.

OPTIONS:
  -q, --query <query>     The query (see SEARCH SYNTAX). Use this form for
                          queries that start with a dash.
  -s, --sort <sort>       Sort keys, comma-separated, each optionally :asc or
                          :desc. Keys: pinned, modified, created, title.
                          (default: pinned,modified)
  -n, --limit <limit>     Return at most this many notes. 0 prints nothing
                          (use --count for the number).
  -o, --offset <offset>   Skip this many notes first. (default: 0)
  -l, --location <location>
                          Which notes: notes, trash, archive or all. (default:
                          notes)
  --count                 Print only how many notes match.
  --format <format>       Output format: tsv, csv or json. (default: tsv)
  --fields <fields>       Fields to print: comma-separated names, or `all`.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

FIELDS:
  Default: id, title, tags, length, matches
  All:     id, title, locked, tags, length, created, modified, pins, location, todos, done, attachments, matches
  Also on request: content (name it, or use --fields all,content).

SEARCH SYNTAX:
  words              all words must occur; `a or b` accepts either
  "a phrase"         the exact phrase
  -term              leave out notes that match the term or filter
  #tag               the tag or its nested children; !#tag only that tag; #*/tag only nested children
  @today @yesterday @last7days     modified then (any number of days for last)
  @date(2026-10-26) @date(<2026-10-26) @date(>2026-10-26)     modified on, before, after
  @ctoday @created7days @cdate(2026-10-26)     the same, by creation date
  @todo @done @task  has open tasks / all tasks checked / has any task
  @tagged @untagged  has at least one tag / has none
  @title             words only match titles
  @pinned            pinned in All Notes
  @images @files @attachments @code     contains that kind of content
  @locked @readonly @empty @untitled    note state
  @wikilinks @backlinks     links to other notes / is linked from other notes
  @ocr               also search text recognised in attachments
  Filters combine: theonotes search "@today @todo meeting"

RESULT:
  The `matches` field counts the text hits in each note; it is 0 for queries without words (such as @today).
  No match: `[]` on stdout with --format json, otherwise `No notes found.` on stderr. The status is 0 either way.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes search "meeting notes"
  theonotes search "@today @todo" --format json
  theonotes search --query "- [ ]" --fields id,title,matches
  theonotes search "@todo" --count

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES CAT

OVERVIEW: Print a note's text.

USAGE: theonotes cat [<note-id>] [--title <title>] [--section <section>] [--offset <offset>] [--limit <limit>] [--format <format>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note. Give this or --title, not both.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  --section <section>     Print one section: a heading line such as `##
                          Tasks`, or an address from `outline`. Excludes
                          --offset and --limit. Interprets \n, \t, \r, \\.
  -o, --offset <offset>   Start at this UTF-8 byte offset.
  -n, --limit <limit>     Upper bound on the UTF-8 bytes printed.
  --format <format>       Output format: tsv, csv or json. (default: tsv)
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

RESULT:
  Plain output is the raw text with no escaping. --format csv: a `content` header and one quoted row.
  --format json: `{"content":"..."}`. A whole-note read adds `"hash"`; a --section read adds `"section"`
  (address, offset, length) and a `"hash"` of that section. `overwrite --base` takes such a hash,
  and reading is the only way to get one. For metadata use `show`.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes cat <note-id>
  theonotes cat --title "Mars"
  theonotes cat <note-id> --section "## Setup"
  theonotes cat <note-id> --offset 0 --limit 500

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES SHOW

OVERVIEW: Print a note's metadata as one row.

USAGE: theonotes show [<note-id>] [--title <title>] [--format <format>] [--fields <fields>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note. Give this or --title, not both.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  --format <format>       Output format: tsv, csv or json. (default: tsv)
  --fields <fields>       Fields to print: comma-separated names, or `all`.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

FIELDS:
  Default: id, title, tags
  All:     id, title, locked, tags, length, created, modified, pins, location, todos, done, attachments
  Also on request: content (name it, or use --fields all,content).

RESULT:
  A locked note shows its metadata, but asking for its content is refused.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes show <note-id>
  theonotes show --title "Mars" --format json --fields all
  theonotes show <note-id> --fields all,content

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES OUTLINE

OVERVIEW: Show a note's sections, each with its address.

USAGE: theonotes outline [<note-id>] [--title <title>] [--section <section>] [--format <format>] [--fields <fields>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note. Give this or --title, not both.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  --section <section>     List only this section and the ones below it: a
                          heading line, or an address from an earlier outline.
                          Interprets \n, \t, \r, \\.
  --format <format>       Output format: tsv, csv or json. (default: tsv)
  --fields <fields>       Fields to print: comma-separated names, or `all`.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

FIELDS:
  Default: address, offset, length
  All:     address, offset, length

RESULT:
  One row per heading and per section preamble, in document order. Every address can be passed to the
  --section option of other commands; in TSV a multi-line address shows its line breaks as \n.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes outline <note-id>
  theonotes outline --title "Cooking notes"
  theonotes outline <note-id> --section "## Usage"
  theonotes outline <note-id> --format json

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES SEARCH-IN

OVERVIEW: Find a string inside one note.

USAGE: theonotes search-in [<note-id>] [--title <title>] [--string <string>] [--context <context>] [--limit <limit>] [--section <section>] [--count] [--format <format>] [--fields <fields>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note. Give this or --title, not both.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  -s, --string, --find <string>
                          Text to look for, ignoring case. Interprets \n, \t,
                          \r, \\.
  --context <context>     Characters of context around each match in its
                          snippet. (default: 120)
  -n, --limit <limit>     Return at most this many matches.
  --section <section>     Search only inside this section (a heading line or
                          an address). Each hit names its section either way.
                          Interprets \n, \t, \r, \\.
  --count                 Print only how many matches there are.
  --format <format>       Output format: tsv, csv or json. (default: tsv)
  --fields <fields>       Fields to print: comma-separated names, or `all`.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

FIELDS:
  Default: offset, snippet, section, source
  All:     offset, snippet, section, source

RESULT:
  Each hit has a snippet and its section (blank in a note without headings). `source` is `body` for text in the note
  or `attachment` for text recognised in an image or PDF; then `offset` points at the attachment's link.
  Snippets use \n \r \t \\ escapes in TSV. No output with status 0 means no match.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes search-in <note-id> --string "TODO"
  theonotes search-in --title "Mars" --string "water" --format json
  theonotes search-in <note-id> --string "TODO" --count
  theonotes search-in <note-id> --string "TODO" --section "## Tasks"

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES CREATE

OVERVIEW: Create a note.

USAGE: theonotes create [<title>] [--content <content>] [--tags <tags>] [--if-not-exists] [--template <template>] [--format <format>] [--fields <fields>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <title>                 Title, written as a `#` heading above the content
                          (below any front matter). Skipped when the content
                          already starts with it. Without a title the first
                          content line serves as the title.

OPTIONS:
  -c, --content <content>
                          The note text; may open with YAML front matter. Read
                          from stdin when absent. Interprets \n, \t, \r, \\.
  --tags <tags>           Comma-separated tags to add. A leading # and
                          whitespace are dropped; nested tags use slashes.
  --if-not-exists         If a note with this title exists, return it instead
                          of creating one. Needs a title.
  --template <template>   Create the note from a template: the title or id of
                          a note tagged #template.
  --format <format>       Output format: tsv, csv or json. (default: tsv)
  --fields <fields>       Fields to print: comma-separated names, or `all`.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

FIELDS:
  Default: id, title, tags
  All:     id, title, locked, tags, length, created, modified, pins, location, todos, done, attachments
  Also on request: content (name it, or use --fields all,content).

NOTES:
  --tags and #tags written in the content are independent and both apply. Prefer --tags: it puts them where
  the tag-position setting says.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes create "My Note" --content "Body text"
  theonotes create --content "# Quick Capture\nSome thoughts"
  theonotes create "My Note" --tags "work,draft" --format json
  printf "line1\nline2" | theonotes create "My Note" --fields id,length

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES APPEND

OVERVIEW: Add text to a note or to one of its sections.

USAGE: theonotes append [<note-id>] [--title <title>] [--content <content>] [--position <position>] [--section <section>] [--no-update-modified]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note. Give this or --title, not both.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  -c, --content <content>
                          Text to add. It always ends on a line of its own, so
                          it cannot run into what follows. Read from stdin
                          when absent. Interprets \n, \t, \r, \\.
  --position <position>   beginning or end. Whole note: beginning is below the
                          title and any leading tags, end is above any
                          trailing tags and footnote definitions. With
                          --section: beginning is right under the heading, end
                          after the last nested subsection. (default: end)
  --section <section>     Add to this section instead of the whole note (a
                          heading line or an address from `outline`).
                          Interprets \n, \t, \r, \\.
  --no-update-modified    Leave the note's modification date as it was.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

RESULT:
  Prints nothing on success; the exit status tells the result. Failures go to stderr.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes append <note-id> --content "New paragraph"
  printf "New content" | theonotes append <note-id>
  theonotes append --title "Mars" --content "Update" --position beginning
  theonotes append <note-id> --section "## Tasks" --content "- [ ] New task"

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES EDIT

OVERVIEW: Replace, insert or delete exact text in a note.

USAGE: theonotes edit [<options>] [<note-id>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note. Give this or --title, not both.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  --section <section>     Confine every --find to this section (a heading line
                          or an address from `outline`). A find may span a
                          heading inside it but not leave it. Interprets \n,
                          \t, \r, \\.
  --find <find>           Exact text to look for. Repeat it, each with its own
                          action, for a batch. Interprets \n, \t, \r, \\.
  --replace <replace>     Text that takes the place of the match. Interprets
                          \n, \t, \r, \\.
  --insert-after <insert-after>
                          Text inserted right after the match. Interprets \n,
                          \t, \r, \\.
  --insert-before <insert-before>
                          Text inserted right before the match. Interprets \n,
                          \t, \r, \\.
  --delete                Delete the match.
  --all                   Act on every match, not only the first.
  --ignore-case           Match ignoring case.
  --word                  Match whole words only (Unicode word boundaries).
  --no-update-modified    Leave the note's modification date as it was.
  --force                 Go ahead even if the write would drop attachments.
                          Without it such a write is refused and the error
                          names the files.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

BATCHES:
  Each --find takes the action that follows it on the command line (--replace, --insert-after,
  --insert-before or --delete), so one call can mix actions. The batch is atomic: if one find fails,
  nothing is written.

ATTACHMENT GATE:
  A write that would drop attachments is refused unless you pass --force; the error lists the files.
  Tag changes are not gated.

RESULT:
  Prints nothing on success; the exit status tells the result. Failures go to stderr.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes edit <note-id> --find "TODO" --replace "DONE"
  theonotes edit <note-id> --find "## Notes" --insert-after "\nNew line"
  theonotes edit <note-id> --find "## Notes" --insert-before "Intro paragraph\n\n"
  theonotes edit <note-id> --find "obsolete paragraph\n" --delete
  theonotes edit <note-id> --find "cat" --replace "dog" --all --word
  theonotes edit <note-id> --find "alpha" --replace "BETA" \
                    --find "draft note\n" --delete \
                    --find "## Notes" --insert-before "Intro\n\n"
  theonotes edit <note-id> --section "## Tasks" --find "done" --replace "DONE"

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES OVERWRITE

OVERVIEW: Replace a note's text, or one section of it.

USAGE: theonotes overwrite [<note-id>] [--title <title>] [--content <content>] [--base <base>] [--section <section>] [--no-update-modified] [--force]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note. Give this or --title, not both.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  -c, --content <content>
                          The new text. Title and tags are derived from it
                          (first heading, #hashtags), so include them. Links
                          to existing attachments must stay or those
                          attachments are dropped. Read from stdin when
                          absent. Interprets \n, \t, \r, \\.
  --base <base>           Hash from an earlier read. The write is refused if
                          the target changed since. Without it the write is
                          unconditional. For a section write, the section's
                          own hash or the hash of the whole note both work.
  --section <section>     Replace this section (heading and all that is nested
                          under it); a heading line or an address from
                          `outline`. The content must start with a heading of
                          the same level. Empty content deletes the section.
                          Interprets \n, \t, \r, \\.
  --no-update-modified    Leave the note's modification date as it was.
  --force                 Go ahead even if the write would drop attachments.
                          Without it such a write is refused and the error
                          names the files.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

NOTES:
  The text replaces the whole note, so title and tags must be part of it. For a section, the heading line is replaced
  too; to replace only the text under it, address `## Heading\npreamble`. A whole-note hash comes from
  `theonotes cat --format json`, a section hash from `theonotes cat --section <address> --format json`.
  The line break before the next heading is kept, so the content need not end in one.
  A write that would drop attachments is refused unless you pass --force; tag changes are not gated.

RESULT:
  Prints nothing on success; the exit status tells the result. Failures go to stderr.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes overwrite <note-id> --base abc1234 --content "# Title\nBody"
  printf "# Title\nBody" | theonotes overwrite <note-id> --base abc1234
  theonotes overwrite <note-id> --content "# Title\nBody"
  theonotes overwrite <note-id> --content "# Title only" --force
  theonotes overwrite <note-id> --section "## Tasks" --base def5678 \
                      --content "## Tasks\nRewritten body"

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES TAGS

OVERVIEW: Work with tags.

USAGE: theonotes tags <subcommand>

OPTIONS:
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

SUBCOMMANDS:
  list (default)          List tags, library-wide or of one note.
  add                     Attach tags to a note.
  remove                  Detach tags from a note.
  rename                  Rename a tag across all notes.
  delete                  Delete a tag from every note.

  Run 'theonotes help tags <subcommand>' for the full help of one subcommand.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES TAGS LIST

OVERVIEW: List tags, library-wide or of one note.

USAGE: theonotes tags list [<note-id>] [--title <title>] [--count] [--format <format>] [--fields <fields>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of a note. Without an id or title the command
                          covers the whole library.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select a note by title (any case). Without a note,
                          the command covers the whole library.
  --count                 Print only how many tags there are.
  --format <format>       Output format: tsv, csv or json. (default: tsv)
  --fields <fields>       Fields to print: comma-separated names, or `all`.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

FIELDS:
  Default: tag
  All:     tag

RESULT:
  Sorted alphabetically, e.g. `#several words#` and `#parent/child`.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes tags list
  theonotes tags list <note-id>
  theonotes tags list --title "Mars" --format json
  theonotes tags list --count

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES TAGS ADD

OVERVIEW: Attach tags to a note.

USAGE: theonotes tags add [<note-id>] [<tags> ...] [--title <title>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note, then the tags to add.
  <tags>                  Tags to add. A leading # and whitespace are dropped;
                          nested tags use slashes; names may contain spaces.
                          Tags already present are skipped.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

RESULT:
  Prints nothing on success; the exit status tells the result. Failures go to stderr.
  Tags go where the tag-position setting says (bottom by default). The modification date is not changed.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes tags add <note-id> work "work/meetings"
  theonotes tags add --title "Mars" favorite

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES TAGS REMOVE

OVERVIEW: Detach tags from a note.

USAGE: theonotes tags remove [<note-id>] [<tags> ...] [--title <title>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note, then the tags to remove.
  <tags>                  Tags to remove. A leading # and whitespace are
                          dropped. Tags the note does not have are skipped.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

RESULT:
  Prints nothing on success; the exit status tells the result. Failures go to stderr.
  The modification date is not changed.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes tags remove <note-id> draft wip
  theonotes tags remove --title "Mars" draft

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES TAGS RENAME

OVERVIEW: Rename a tag across all notes.

USAGE: theonotes tags rename [<old>] [--from <from>] [<new>] [--to <to>] [--force]

ARGUMENTS:
  <old>                   Tag to rename. A leading # and whitespace are
                          dropped.
  <new>                   The new name. A leading # and whitespace are
                          dropped.

OPTIONS:
  --from <from>           Same as the <old> argument.
  --to <to>               Same as the <new> argument.
  --force                 Merge when the new name already exists. Without it
                          such a rename is refused, because a merge cannot be
                          undone.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

RESULT:
  Prints nothing on success; the exit status tells the result. Failures go to stderr.
  Nested children follow the rename. A tag that does not exist is a no-op. Refused when the new name exists
  (unless --force) and when any affected note is locked. Modification dates are not changed.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes tags rename work job
  theonotes tags rename --from draft --to published
  theonotes tags rename old-tag existing-tag --force

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES TAGS DELETE

OVERVIEW: Delete a tag from every note.

USAGE: theonotes tags delete [<tag>] [--name <name>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <tag>                   Tag to delete. A leading # and whitespace are
                          dropped.

OPTIONS:
  --name <name>           Same as the <tag> argument.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

RESULT:
  Prints nothing on success; the exit status tells the result. Failures go to stderr.
  Nested children are deleted with it. A tag that does not exist is a no-op. Refused when any affected note is
  locked. Modification dates are not changed.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes tags delete draft
  theonotes tags delete --name "work/old"

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES ATTACHMENTS

OVERVIEW: Work with a note's attachments.

USAGE: theonotes attachments <subcommand>

OPTIONS:
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

SUBCOMMANDS:
  list (default)          List a note's attachments.
  add                     Attach bytes read from stdin; a link is appended to
                          the note.
  add-url                 Attach a file downloaded from an https URL; a link
                          is appended.
  delete                  Delete an attachment and its link.
  save                    Write an attachment's bytes to stdout.

  Run 'theonotes help attachments <subcommand>' for the full help of one subcommand.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES ATTACHMENTS LIST

OVERVIEW: List a note's attachments.

USAGE: theonotes attachments list [<note-id>] [--title <title>] [--format <format>] [--fields <fields>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note. Give this or --title, not both.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  --format <format>       Output format: tsv, csv or json. (default: tsv)
  --fields <fields>       Fields to print: comma-separated names, or `all`.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

FIELDS:
  Default: filename, size
  All:     filename, size

RESULT:
  `size` is in bytes. No output means the note has no attachments.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes attachments list <note-id>
  theonotes attachments list --title "Mars" --format json

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES ATTACHMENTS ADD

OVERVIEW: Attach bytes read from stdin; a link is appended to the note.

USAGE: theonotes attachments add [<note-id>] [--title <title>] [--filename <filename>] [--no-update-modified]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note. Give this or --title, not both.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  -f, --filename <filename>
                          Name for the attachment (required).
  --no-update-modified    Leave the note's modification date as it was.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

RESULT:
  A Markdown link to the file is appended to the note. If the name is taken, a numbered variant is used
  (`photo.jpg` becomes `photo 2.jpg`) and the link follows it.
  Prints nothing on success; the exit status tells the result. Failures go to stderr.

EXAMPLES:
  cat photo.jpg | theonotes attachments add <note-id> --filename photo.jpg
  theonotes attachments add <note-id> --filename photo.jpg < photo.jpg

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES ATTACHMENTS ADD-URL

OVERVIEW: Attach a file downloaded from an https URL; a link is appended.

USAGE: theonotes attachments add-url [<note-id>] [--title <title>] [--url <url>] [--filename <filename>] [--no-update-modified]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note. Give this or --title, not both.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  -u, --url <url>         The https URL to download (required).
  -f, --filename <filename>
                          Name for the attachment. Defaults to the last path
                          segment of the URL.
  --no-update-modified    Leave the note's modification date as it was.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

RESULT:
  The scheme must be https://; http://, file:// and data: are refused. A Markdown link to the file is appended;
  if the name is taken, a numbered variant is used (`photo 2.jpg`) and the link follows it.
  Prints nothing on success; the exit status tells the result. Failures go to stderr.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes attachments add-url <note-id> --url https://example.com/photo.jpg
  theonotes attachments add-url <note-id> --url https://example.com/raw --filename photo.jpg

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES ATTACHMENTS DELETE

OVERVIEW: Delete an attachment and its link.

USAGE: theonotes attachments delete [<note-id>] [--title <title>] [--filename <filename>] [--no-update-modified]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note. Give this or --title, not both.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  -f, --filename <filename>
                          Name of the attachment file to delete (required).
  --no-update-modified    Leave the note's modification date as it was.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

RESULT:
  The file is deleted and its link is removed from the note text; no follow-up edit is needed.
  Prints nothing on success; the exit status tells the result. Failures go to stderr.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes attachments delete <note-id> --filename photo.jpg

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES ATTACHMENTS SAVE

OVERVIEW: Write an attachment's bytes to stdout.

USAGE: theonotes attachments save [<note-id>] [--title <title>] [--filename <filename>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note. Give this or --title, not both.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  -f, --filename <filename>
                          Name of the attachment file to write out (required).
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

RESULT:
  Raw bytes on stdout. Refused when stdout is a terminal, so redirect it. To get text, pipe through base64.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes attachments save <note-id> --filename photo.jpg > photo.jpg

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES PIN

OVERVIEW: Work with pins.

USAGE: theonotes pin <subcommand>

OPTIONS:
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

SUBCOMMANDS:
  list (default)          Show where one note is pinned, or every pin context
                          currently in use.
  add                     Pin a note in All Notes and/or within tags.
  remove                  Unpin a note from All Notes and/or tags.

  Run 'theonotes help pin <subcommand>' for the full help of one subcommand.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES PIN LIST

OVERVIEW: Show where one note is pinned, or every pin context currently in use.

USAGE: theonotes pin list [<note-id>] [--title <title>] [--format <format>] [--fields <fields>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of a note. Without an id or title the command
                          covers the whole library.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select a note by title (any case). Without a note,
                          the command covers the whole library.
  --format <format>       Output format: tsv, csv or json. (default: tsv)
  --fields <fields>       Fields to print: comma-separated names, or `all`.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

FIELDS:
  Default: pin
  All:     pin

RESULT:
  `global` is the All Notes pin; `#tag` entries are pins within that tag. With a note: where it is pinned, and
  no output means it is not pinned. Without a note: every context in use, and no output means nothing is pinned.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes pin list
  theonotes pin list <note-id>
  theonotes pin list --title "Mars" --format json

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES PIN ADD

OVERVIEW: Pin a note in All Notes and/or within tags.

USAGE: theonotes pin add [<note-id>] [<targets> ...] [--title <title>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note, then the pin targets.
  <targets>               Use `global` for the All Notes pin, tag names for
                          tag pins, or both (a leading # and whitespace are
                          ignored). Every tag has to exist, or no pin is
                          added.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

RESULT:
  Prints nothing on success; the exit status tells the result. Failures go to stderr.
  Pins that already exist are skipped.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes pin add <note-id> global
  theonotes pin add <note-id> work projects
  theonotes pin add --title "Mars" global work

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES PIN REMOVE

OVERVIEW: Unpin a note from All Notes and/or tags.

USAGE: theonotes pin remove [<note-id>] [<targets> ...] [--title <title>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note, then the pin targets.
  <targets>               `global` and/or tag names to unpin from (a leading #
                          and whitespace are dropped). All tags must exist,
                          otherwise nothing is removed.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

RESULT:
  Prints nothing on success; the exit status tells the result. Failures go to stderr.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes pin remove <note-id> global
  theonotes pin remove <note-id> work
  theonotes pin remove --title "Mars" global work

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES TRASH

OVERVIEW: Send a note to the trash.

USAGE: theonotes trash [<note-id>] [--title <title>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note. Give this or --title, not both.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

RESULT:
  Prints nothing on success; the exit status tells the result. Failures go to stderr.
  The note stays recoverable: `theonotes restore` brings it back.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes trash <note-id>
  theonotes trash --title "Old Note"

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES ARCHIVE

OVERVIEW: Send a note to the archive.

USAGE: theonotes archive [<note-id>] [--title <title>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note. Give this or --title, not both.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

RESULT:
  Prints nothing on success; the exit status tells the result. Failures go to stderr.
  The note leaves the active list but is kept; `theonotes restore` moves it back.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes archive <note-id>
  theonotes archive --title "Finished Project"

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES RESTORE

OVERVIEW: Bring a note back from the trash or archive.

USAGE: theonotes restore [<note-id>] [--title <title>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note. Give this or --title, not both.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

RESULT:
  Prints nothing on success; the exit status tells the result. Failures go to stderr.
  A note that is already in the active list is left alone.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes restore <note-id>
  theonotes restore --title "Recovered Draft"

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES APP

OVERVIEW: Talk to the running app.

USAGE: theonotes app <subcommand>

OPTIONS:
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

SUBCOMMANDS:
  open                    Show a note in the app.
  get-selection           Print what is selected in the note open in the app.

  Run 'theonotes help app <subcommand>' for the full help of one subcommand.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES APP OPEN

OVERVIEW: Show a note in the app.

USAGE: theonotes app open [<note-id>] [--title <title>] [--header <header>] [--edit] [--new-window] [--float] [--selection-text <selection-text>] [--selection-offset <selection-offset>] [--selection-length <selection-length>]

ARGUMENTS:
  <note-id>               Id of the note. Give this or --title, not both.

OPTIONS:
  -t, --title <title>     Select the note by title (any case) instead of by
                          id.
  --header <header>       Heading inside the note to scroll to.
  --edit                  Put the caret in the editor. Ignored when a
                          selection is set.
  --new-window            Open the note in its own window.
  --float                 Show the note in a floating panel that stays above
                          other windows.
  --selection-text <selection-text>
                          Text to select once the note is open. Case-
                          sensitive; it must occur exactly once. Excludes
                          --selection-offset.
  --selection-offset <selection-offset>
                          UTF-8 byte offset where the selection starts.
                          Excludes --selection-text.
  --selection-length <selection-length>
                          Selection length in UTF-8 bytes; 0 or absent gives a
                          caret. Used only with --selection-offset.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

RESULT:
  Prints nothing on success; the exit status tells the result. Failures go to stderr.
  The app is brought to the front with the note open.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes app open <note-id>
  theonotes app open --title "Mars" --header "Moons" --edit
  theonotes app open --title "Mars" --selection-text "Phobos"
  theonotes app open <note-id> --new-window

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES APP GET-SELECTION

OVERVIEW: Print what is selected in the note open in the app.

USAGE: theonotes app get-selection [--format <format>] [--fields <fields>]

OPTIONS:
  --format <format>       Output format: tsv, csv or json. (default: tsv)
  --fields <fields>       Fields to print: comma-separated names, or `all`.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

FIELDS:
  Default: id, title, selectionOffset, selectionLength, selectionText
  All:     id, title, locked, tags, length, created, modified, pins, location, todos, done, attachments, selectionOffset, selectionLength, selectionText
  Also on request: content (name it, or use --fields all,content).

RESULT:
  Both selection values count UTF-8 bytes; a caret is a selection of length 0. Empty output (TSV, CSV) or `{}`
  (JSON) with status 0 means no note is open. A locked note reports its metadata but no selection or content.

EXAMPLES:
  theonotes app get-selection
  theonotes app get-selection --fields selectionOffset,selectionLength,selectionText
  theonotes app get-selection --format json --fields all,content

------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEONOTES MCP-SERVER

OVERVIEW: Serve the library to MCP clients over stdio.

USAGE: theonotes mcp-server [--only-tags <only-tags>] [--exclude-tags <exclude-tags>]

OPTIONS:
  --only-tags <only-tags>
                          Comma-separated tags. Serve only notes with one of
                          these tags or a nested child.
  --exclude-tags <exclude-tags>
                          Comma-separated tags. Hide notes with one of these
                          tags or a nested child. Wins over --only-tags.
  -h, --help              Show help for this command.

SETUP:
  Point an MCP client at `theonotes mcp-server`. It speaks JSON-RPC 2.0, one JSON message per line on stdin and stdout.
  The tools cover the same operations as the CLI, with deliberate differences: overwrite_note requires `baseHash`,
  attachment removals are declared with `expectedRemovedAttachments` (the CLI has --force), and only the CLI has
  --no-update-modified. Every tool carries readOnlyHint and destructiveHint annotations, so a client can ask the user
  before a destructive call.

SCOPES:
  --only-tags and --exclude-tags (or the variables below) confine the server to the notes those tags select.
  Lists and searches show in-scope notes only, and a call that names a note outside the scope fails with `out_of_scope`.
  With exactly one --only-tags value, create_note adds that tag itself; with several, the call must name one of them.
  rename_tag and delete_tag refuse tags that define the scope. Use one server entry per scope in the client's config.

ADDITIONS:
  create_note_from_template   An extra tool: creates a note from a template (the title or id of a note tagged #template).
  capture_thought   An extra tool: saves the given text as a new note, unchanged.
  THEONOTES_MCP_ONLY_TAGS     Same as --only-tags when the flag is absent.
  THEONOTES_MCP_EXCLUDE_TAGS  Same as --exclude-tags when the flag is absent.

PRIVACY:
  The server reads and writes the local library in place. It sends nothing over the network, except that
  add_attachment_from_url downloads the URL it is given. There is no telemetry.
"""##

    /// `theonotes -h` / `theonotes help`: the overview.
    static let overview: String = ##"""
OVERVIEW: Read and write your notes from the command line.

USAGE: theonotes [--version] <subcommand>

OPTIONS:
  -v, --version           Print the version.
  -h, --help              Show help.

SUBCOMMANDS:
  list                    List notes, narrowed by location or one tag.
  search                  Find notes with the search query language.
  cat                     Print a note's text.
  show                    Print a note's metadata.
  outline                 Show a note's sections, each with its address.
  search-in               Find a string inside one note.
  create                  Create a note.
  append                  Add text to a note or to one of its sections.
  edit                    Replace, insert or delete exact text in a note.
  overwrite               Replace a note's text, or one section of it.
  tags                    Work with tags.
  attachments             Work with a note's attachments.
  pin                     Work with pins.
  trash                   Send a note to the trash.
  archive                 Send a note to the archive.
  restore                 Bring a note back from the trash or archive.
  app                     Talk to the running app.
  mcp-server              Serve the library to MCP clients over stdio.

HELP:
  theonotes -h, theonotes help       This overview.
  theonotes help <subcommand>        Help for one command.
  theonotes help all                 Every command's help in one document.
  theonotes --generate-completion-script <shell>
                                     Completion script for zsh, bash or fish.

OUTPUT:
  --format tsv     Tab-separated rows without a header (default).
  --format csv     RFC 4180 rows with a header.
  --format json    One JSON document on stdout, errors included.
  --fields a,b     Pick fields: comma-separated names, or `all`. The defaults depend on the command.

  JSON shapes: a list of results is `[{...},{...}]` (empty: `[]`), a single result is `{...}`,
  an error is `{"error":{"code":"...","message":"..."}}` on stdout.
  Exceptions: cat gives `{"content":"..."}`, attachments save gives
  `{"filename":...,"size":N,"base64":"..."}`.

  Commands that change something print nothing on success and take no --format;
  the exit status is the result. MCP tools return structured results for writes.

NOTE FIELDS:
  id, title, locked, tags, length (UTF-8 bytes), created, modified, pins, location
  (notes, trash or archive), todos (open tasks), done (checked tasks), attachments.
  `content` is only printed when named, and `all` does not include it: use `--fields all,content`.

CONVENTIONS:
  Exit status: 0 success, 1 the operation failed, 64 the command line is wrong.
  Errors: plain text on stderr; with --format json a JSON object on stdout. The status is the same.
  Tags: #one, #several words#, #parent/child. The leading # is optional on input.
  Timestamps: ISO 8601 in UTC (YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ).
  Escapes: TSV cells and text options use \n \r \t \\. Text read from stdin is taken literally.
  --content: read from stdin when the option is absent.
  Notes are selected by id or by --title (any case).
  Locked notes never give up their text through the CLI.

SECTION ADDRESSES:
  A section is a heading and everything below it. Its address is the heading line as written,
  `#` marks included; case matters, spacing does not. Several heading lines joined by `\n`
  spell out a path from an outer heading to an inner one. A heading that occurs twice needs
  its parents, or a last line holding a 1-based number that picks the Nth match. A last line
  `preamble` means the text under a heading, before its first subheading. `theonotes outline`
  lists every address of a note, and a wrong address is answered with candidates.
    "## Usage"                the one section with that heading
    "# Guide\n## Usage"       `## Usage` below `# Guide`
    "# Guide\n## Usage\n2"    the second `## Usage` below `# Guide`
    "## Usage\npreamble"      the text under `## Usage`, before its first subheading

MARKDOWN:
  Notes are Markdown with these extras: ==highlight== (==🔴text== and 🔵 🟢 🟡 🟣 for colours),
  ~underline~, ~~strikethrough~~, #tags, [[Note Title]] and [[Note Title|label]] links,
  $inline$ and $$block$$ math, - [ ] and - [x] tasks, pipe tables (a pipe at both ends of every row),
  callouts (> [!NOTE], also TIP, IMPORTANT, WARNING, CAUTION) and YAML front matter on the first line.
  Switched off: setext headings, indented code blocks and lazy continuation. Long lines soft-wrap.

GLOBAL OPTIONS:
  --data-dir <path>       Use the library in <path> (also THEONOTES_DATA_DIR).
"""##

    /// What `theonotes help <unknown command>` prints: the subcommand list of the overview.
    static let subcommandList: String = ##"""
